<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sayfawa dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sayfawa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sayfawa dynasty was the ruling house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>, which claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-ibn-dhi-yazan">Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</a>. It ruled for many centuries, making it one of the longest-lasting dynasties in African history, until it was displaced in the nineteenth century. Accounts of the Kanem-Bornu Empire credit the dynasty's continuity with providing stable royal legitimacy under the title of mai.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty.jpg" alt="Sayfawa dynasty" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Finden, Edward Francis, 1791-1857 -- Engraver · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Council_of_the_Sultan_of_Bornou_1826.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
